WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.039
MCP exposes capabilities through exactly three primitives, tools, resources, and prompts.

2
00:00:07.599 --> 00:00:13.039
Once you understand each one and who controls it, you know how to model any capability on an

3
00:00:13.039 --> 00:00:18.239
MCP server. In this lesson, we'll define each primitive, see what makes them different,

4
00:00:18.239 --> 00:00:26.799
and build intuition for which one to use when. Every capability an MCP server exposes is a tool,

5
00:00:26.799 --> 00:00:33.200
a resource, or a prompt. And what separates them isn't what they do, but who decides to use them.

6
00:00:33.759 --> 00:00:40.080
An MCP tool is a callable function the model invokes. An MCP resource is a data object

7
00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:47.439
identified by URI, which the application injects into context. An MCP prompt is a reusable,

8
00:00:47.439 --> 00:00:53.119
parameterized template the user selects to start an interaction. Notice the pattern,

9
00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:58.799
each primitive has a different owner. That ownership distinction is the single most

10
00:00:58.799 --> 00:01:05.120
important thing to internalize here, and it's a concept the Architect Foundation's exam tests

11
00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:11.360
directly. Model controls tools, application controls resources, user controls prompts.

12
00:01:11.919 --> 00:01:18.720
Memorize that split and you've got the core of the primitives topic locked down. Tools are the

13
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:24.160
most active primitive. When you expose a function as an MCP tool, you're giving the model the

14
00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:30.239
ability to do something, such as run a database query, send a notification, call a calculation

15
00:01:30.239 --> 00:01:36.239
service, or trigger any other side-affecting operation. Each tool gets a JSON schema that

16
00:01:36.239 --> 00:01:42.800
describes its name, required inputs, types, and what it returns. Claude reads that schema and

17
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:47.760
uses it to understand how to invoke the tool correctly. Then it reasons about whether calling

18
00:01:47.839 --> 00:01:54.319
the tool actually serves the user's goal in this conversation. The key word is decides. The model

19
00:01:54.319 --> 00:02:00.080
is in control of when and whether the tool gets called. That's the practical meaning of model

20
00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:06.720
controlled. Resources work differently from tools. They're data sources that the server exposes for

21
00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:12.800
the model to read. Files, database records, configuration documents, and knowledge bases.

22
00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:18.399
The application injects them into Claude's context before or during a conversation. The

23
00:02:18.399 --> 00:02:24.160
server registers each resource with a URI so clients can address and request it. But the

24
00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:30.320
decision about which resources to include in context isn't made by the model. The host application

25
00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:36.320
makes that call based on its own logic about what data should be present. So if you've got a

26
00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:41.600
knowledge base that should always be in context when a user asks a product question, that's a

27
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:48.720
resource. The app loads it automatically, not because Claude asked for it. The comparison between

28
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:55.440
tools and resources comes down to control and dynamism. Tools are model controlled and action

29
00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:01.839
oriented. Claude uses them when the conversation warrants it based on its own in-context reasoning.

30
00:03:01.839 --> 00:03:07.440
Resources are application controlled and data oriented. The host loads them based on its own

31
00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:13.119
business logic, completely independent of what the model thinks it needs. Here's a practical

32
00:03:13.119 --> 00:03:18.880
heuristic. A weather lookup the model calls on demand because the user asked about the weather.

33
00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:24.720
That's a tool. A company policy document, the application always loads into context before

34
00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:30.080
any compliance related conversation. That's a resource. When you're designing a server, just

35
00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:37.119
ask yourself, who makes the decision to use this, the model, or the application? The ownership model

36
00:03:37.119 --> 00:03:43.039
is worth sitting with a bit longer because the three-way split isn't arbitrary. It's principled.

37
00:03:43.039 --> 00:03:49.119
The model controls tools because it's the one reasoning about what action needs to happen next

38
00:03:49.119 --> 00:03:55.679
in a conversation, and it has the most context for that decision. The application controls resources

39
00:03:55.679 --> 00:04:01.520
because it has the broader system level context to know what data should be present for a given

40
00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:08.000
workflow or user state. The user controls prompts because prompts are really about letting humans

41
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:15.039
configure their own starting point intentionally before an interaction begins. MCP landed on this

42
00:04:15.039 --> 00:04:21.519
split deliberately. Each ownership model maps to the party with the most relevant information to

43
00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:28.399
make that specific decision correctly, safely, and without overstepping. Prompts are the third

44
00:04:28.399 --> 00:04:34.000
primitive. They're reusable, parameterized templates stored on the server that users can

45
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:40.000
browse and explicitly apply. Think of them as pre-built workflows a user can launch by name.

46
00:04:40.559 --> 00:04:46.320
The mental model, a user opens a chat interface, sees a list of prompts the connected server makes

47
00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:52.160
available, picks one, fills in any required variables, and the interaction starts with that

48
00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:58.559
template already applied and active. The user is explicitly initiating this. The model doesn't

49
00:04:58.559 --> 00:05:04.079
choose to apply a prompt on its own, and the application doesn't inject it silently without

50
00:05:04.079 --> 00:05:10.160
the user knowing. The human makes a deliberate, intentional selection. That's the practical meaning

51
00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:17.279
of user-controlled for this particular primitive. When you're designing an MCP server, the primitive

52
00:05:17.279 --> 00:05:22.799
choice is a real design decision with real consequences for how your server gets used

53
00:05:22.799 --> 00:05:28.799
and misused. Use a tool for actions or computations the model should trigger during its reasoning,

54
00:05:28.799 --> 00:05:34.799
such as search queries, calculations, and mutation operations that change state. Use a resource for

55
00:05:34.799 --> 00:05:40.000
background data the application should load and inject before the model generates a response.

56
00:05:40.559 --> 00:05:46.239
Use a prompt for reusable starting point workflows that make sense for users to select intentionally

57
00:05:46.239 --> 00:05:52.640
before beginning a task. The reliable shortcut is always to ask who drives this decision. If it's the

58
00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:58.959
model, it's a tool. If it's the application, it's a resource. If it's the user, it's a prompt.

59
00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:05.920
People tend to lump tools and resources together because both end up as information that the model

60
00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:12.079
uses. The real split is who pulls the trigger. Claude reaches for a tool because the conversation

61
00:06:12.079 --> 00:06:19.040
calls for it. Your application pushes a resource in because its own logic says to do so. Once you

62
00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:24.880
sort capabilities by who decides rather than by what comes back, server design stops being

63
00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:29.040
guesswork. That wraps up this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

